# BSA M20 500 cc motorcycle with sidecar, 1939-1945.
#
# The BSA WD M20: the 500 cc side-valve single, the most numerous British
# military motorcycle of the Second World War, here in combination form with
# a sidecar. The M20 was built mostly without sidecar lugs; the purpose-built
# outfit was the M21, already in the tree. No period photograph of an M20
# with sidecar was found, so the machine is the M20 the pack photographs and
# the chair is a period-correct combination recorded as such.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSA_M20
#
# Dimensions: solo machine 2.179 x 0.739 m on a 1.372 m wheelbase
# (autoevolution); a second published set gives 2.083 x 0.787 x 1.041 m.
# Outfit width is not published; the chair width is a period estimate.
#
# Shape reference: image 1 is a true level right-side elevation of a 1944
# M20; image 2 is the WD census-numbered machine; images 3-4 are a G14 outfit
# and a 1956 M21 outfit, labelled as comparables, not as M20 outfits.
#
# Colours: WD khaki green with a white census number and blackout lamp. One
# colourway. Weight 120, provisional.
#
# Speed 70 km/h. Comfort 25/338: an open chair with an exposed rider.
# Classes 0,1.
# Dates are road presence: 1939 intro, retires 1958.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/bsa-m20-combo.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/bsa-m20-combo.dae. References:
# citycars/references/bsa-m20-combo/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons and
# period photographs).
#
# Accepted deviations: there is no confirmed period photograph of an exact M20
# outfit. The chair and the inferred mounting points are based on comparable
# machines, not evidence of a standard WD M20 combination. Solo height and
# outfit width are estimates. Stage B lateral shift +0.3153 pak units, so that
# the motorcycle rather than the combined bounding box sits at the tile centre.


obj=citycar
name=BSAM20Combo(KhakiGreen)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=120
intro_year=1939
intro_month=1
retire_year=1958
retire_month=6
speed=70
length=2
class=0,1
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=338

